{ Original Hippopotamus as Black course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Nf3 a6 5. Bd3 b6 6. O-O Bb7 7. Re1 e6 8. a4 Nd7 9. h3 h6 10. Be3 Ne7 { Plan: Decide on king safety and a central break. Examine ...c5 against d4 and ...d5 against e4; neither move is compulsory or safe in every version. Opponent resource: White owns more space and can prepare d5, e5 or a wing attack. Finishing the diagram is not a strategic achievement if Black then has no active move. } *

[Event "HB-02 White chooses Be2"]
[Site "Original study material"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White study side"]
[Black "Black study side"]
[Result "*"]

{ Original Hippopotamus as Black course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Nf3 a6 5. Be2 b6 6. O-O Bb7 7. Re1 e6 { Authored anchor. The bishop on e2 is less directly aimed at h7 than on d3, but White can support a central advance without exposing pieces. Black preserves flexibility. } 8. Be3 Nf6 9. e5 dxe5 10. Nxe5 O-O { Plan: Develop ...Nd7 and assess ...Ne7 or ...Nf6 from the actual center. If a central exchange opens a bishop diagonal, calculate it before making another wing pawn move. Opponent resource: White may use Bf1, a4 and d5 to squeeze. A lack of immediate threats is not permission for unlimited waiting. } *

